          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $62,358 median income with a 30% renter rate and 22.1% college attainment, NC-HD-5 is a district of economically constrained working adults for whom housing costs, healthcare access, and job quality are the primary governance concerns. In a district where one-third of households rent rather than own, housing affordability and tenant protections are not abstract policy debates—they are direct household budget issues that shape whether young adults stay in the district and whether working families experience economic stability. A representative who speaks with specificity to affordable housing policy, workforce development in vocational trades, and healthcare cost management for lower-income households is addressing the material concerns that define this constituency's evaluation of whether government is delivering for them." },
          { name: "Black Voter Engagement & Community Leadership", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 36.53% of the district population, Black voters are the second-largest demographic and represent a constituency whose support is neither automatic nor guaranteed—the 46% opposition in a 54% Republican district may well be concentrated in or disproportionately drawn from Black households and neighborhoods. Authentic engagement with Black-led institutions, Black small business owners, and Black community leaders on economic opportunity, healthcare access, criminal justice, and educational attainment is not peripheral to this district's politics—it is central. A representative who builds visible, ongoing relationships with Black constituents and delivers on their specific governance concerns commands credibility that consolidates the margin; one who treats the Black community as a bloc to be managed rather than as constituents with direct governance stakes will find that 54% margin compressed by opposition mobilization." },
          { name: "Renter Constituency & Housing Stability", tag: "careful", why: "The 30% renter rate in NC-HD-5 represents roughly one-third of the district's households, and renters are statistically younger, more likely to be Black or Hispanic, and more responsive to messages about housing affordability, tenant protections, and economic mobility than homeowner majorities typically are. In a district this economically constrained, renters can represent a swing constituency if mobilized around specific housing and economic concerns. Any candidate who speaks only to homeowner concerns—property tax management, neighborhood stability, school quality—while remaining silent on renter issues is leaving a swing constituency unengaged and is vulnerable to an opposition candidate who offers concrete housing affordability solutions." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Stable Republican Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 result in a district with a 36.53% Black population, 30% renter rate, and working-class $62,358 median income is not a comfortable Republican majority—it is a contested margin that reflects a constituency actively evaluating whether the incumbent is delivering economic results. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 54% represents a safe hold or assumes that base consolidation is sufficient has misread the district's composition and competitive reality. The 46% opposition in a genuinely diverse, economically constrained district is a warning signal that the margin is contingent on specific constituent attention and economic performance, not inherited partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Ignoring Renter Economic Concerns While Focusing Only on Homeowner Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where renters comprise 30% of households and are likely to be disproportionately younger and more responsive to economic messaging, a legislative agenda focused exclusively on homeowner concerns—property tax cuts, school choice, neighborhood stability—while remaining silent on affordable housing, tenant protections, and housing voucher adequacy will cede the renter constituency to an opposition candidate with a concrete housing platform. This is not a rhetorical vulnerability; it is a specific pathway for the 46% opposition to organize and grow by offering solutions the incumbent is not articulating." }
