          { name: "Treating a 61-39 Margin as Electorally Safe", tag: "avoid", why: "A 22-point headline margin in a district where white voters comprise only 54% of the population and renters are 42% is not arithmetically as safe as it appears. The margin depends on specific conditions — white Republican consolidation and Democratic underperformance among Black and renter voters — that are contingent on incumbent performance, challenger profile, and turnout dynamics. Any campaign that treats 61% as a durable floor and neglects the opportunity costs of allowing a credible challenger to mobilize the 32.55% Black population and younger renter base is making a strategic error. The seat can shift significantly if opposition mobilization is effective." },
          { name: "Assuming Low Educational Attainment Means Low Voter Sophistication or Low Issue Salience", tag: "avoid", why: "The 22.2% college attainment rate signals a working-class constituency, not a politically disengaged one. Working-class voters in HD-10 are highly attuned to material economic concerns — jobs, wages, housing, healthcare — and evaluate candidates on whether they are delivering on those concrete issues rather than abstract ideological positioning. Any campaign that responds to low college attainment by relying on partisan identity messaging or avoiding substantive discussion of local economic conditions will miss the opportunity to consolidate working-class support where the incumbent's record of delivery (or failure) is the determining factor. This is a constituency that demands specificity, not platitudes." }
