          "The Hispanic population at 17.56% is the largest single minority group in HD-14 and represents both the most significant swing constituency and the clearest signal of where the district's demographic future points. In a district this young, with this much rental dependency, and this modest income, Hispanic voters' evaluation of whether government is delivering on housing costs, wage growth, healthcare access, and economic mobility for working families will determine whether the current 66-34 margin holds or contracts. The remaining demographic gap — roughly 7-8% unaccounted for by the four reported categories — likely includes additional Hispanic or Latino residents not fully captured in Census reporting or mixed-race/multiethnic respondents who do not sort neatly into single categories. Any candidate, regardless of party, who assumes the 66% result is stable without specifically addressing why working-class young adults and Hispanic voters should invest in their governance is operating with incomplete strategic intelligence. The seat is currently Republican by a wide margin, but that margin is not structural — it is relational, dependent on whether the incumbent or challenger is perceived as delivering for people whose primary economic concern is whether they can afford rent and build economic security in a high-cost environment."
