          { name: "African American Community Engagement and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 27.8% of the district, the Black community represents more than one in four residents — a constituency large enough that its mobilization or demobilization determines the margin in a 61-39 district. The combination of 17.5% college attainment, $49,930 income, and the Piedmont's post-industrial economic transition creates specific concerns around workforce development, small business access to capital, and whether economic recovery is reaching Black households at the same pace as others. Greensboro's history of civic activism and its significant Black middle class and entrepreneurial community mean that authentic engagement on economic opportunity — not token acknowledgment — is both a governing obligation and an electoral necessity." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Integration and Workforce Issues", tag: "careful", why: "At 17.17% of the district, the Hispanic community is the fastest-growing demographic segment in the Piedmont and represents a constituency whose economic and civic concerns are often subsumed into broader working-class narratives. Agricultural and service-sector workforce issues, access to healthcare and education for immigrant families, and whether economic policy is addressing the specific barriers Hispanic workers face are constituent needs that deserve explicit attention rather than assumption. In a 61-39 district, the Hispanic community's political trajectory — whether it consolidates into the existing Republican base, aligns with the Democratic opposition, or remains politically fluid — is a material factor in future electoral competitiveness." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61-39 margin in a working-class district with 28.3% renters, modest income, and a 27.8% Black community is comfortable but not fireproof — particularly if the incumbent relies on consolidating existing voters rather than expanding the Republican coalition. The combination of economic pressure on working-class households and the demographic reality of a large and politically fluid Black and Hispanic constituency means that any complacency about the margin or neglect of opposition constituencies' material concerns creates space for a credible challenger to build." },
          { name: "Positioning Fiscal Conservatism as the Answer to Working-Class Economic Insecurity", tag: "avoid", why: "At $49,930 income, 28.3% renter rate, and 17.5% college attainment, the district's primary concerns are immediate economic security — healthcare access, wage adequacy, and housing stability — not abstract budget philosophy. Messaging that prioritizes deficit reduction or limited government without addressing how those principles will concretely improve the material lives of working-class households, renters, and Black and Hispanic families will read as disconnected from constituent reality and will activate opposition mobilization among those experiencing economic precarity." }
