          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $47,432 median income with 35.3% renters, this district's majority are economically vulnerable households where housing costs consume a disproportionate share of income and eviction risk is real. Renter protection ordinances, affordable housing development, rent stabilization, and tenant rights enforcement are not peripheral policy concerns but primary constituent needs affecting more than one in three district residents. A candidate who speaks with specificity and commitment to housing as an economic justice issue — not charity — commands credibility with the material reality of this constituency's daily life." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Affordability in a Lower-Income District", tag: "lean-into", why: "A median household income of $47,432 combined with a 43.8-year median age signals a constituency facing both healthcare affordability pressure and age-related health needs on constrained budgets. Medicaid expansion, prescription drug cost controls, rural health access (if applicable to district geography), and primary care availability are the healthcare issues this population actually evaluates on. A candidate whose healthcare positioning is grounded in cost reduction and access expansion — rather than abstract market rhetoric — reflects how working-age and older adults in lower-income households actually experience the healthcare system." },
          { name: "College Attainment Gap & Youth Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "At 16.6% college-educated, NC-HD-23 has the lowest education attainment profile and signals a district where families have limited access to post-secondary pathways and where youth face real barriers to upward mobility. Community college funding, technical training capacity, apprenticeship programs, and K-12 resource adequacy are the educational equity concerns for families in this district. The tension here is that education is both a core Democratic constituency value and a real area where incumbent performance can be measured — parents know whether their local schools have adequate resources, and a challenger can credibly position an education agenda as more robust than incumbency." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as an Immovable Democratic Base", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 margin in a district with this economic profile — low income, high renter rate, low education attainment — is not a floor but a current position held by alignment between Democratic branding and the district's Black majority community. If the incumbent is perceived as passive on housing, healthcare costs, or job quality, or if a challenger mobilizes around economic populism or delivers concrete results on these concerns, the 56% is vulnerable. Any incumbent or candidate who assumes this seat because of demographics rather than active constituent engagement and performance is vulnerable to a credible alternative." },
          { name: "Dismissing Renter Concerns as Lower-Income Issue Drift", tag: "avoid", why: "At 35.3% renters in a low-income district, housing instability is not a fringe issue or a specialty constituency concern — it is a primary economic reality for more than one in three district residents. A candidate or incumbent who frames renter protection as secondary to other priorities, or who defers housing policy to abstract market solutions, is ignoring the material circumstance of a plurality of constituents. The opposition can exploit this framing gap by offering specific renter-focused proposals while the incumbent offers general economic messaging." }
