          { name: "Education Funding and Economic Mobility Through Education", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 58.4% college attainment — significantly above state and national averages — HD-30's constituency has invested heavily in education and expects return on that investment through economic opportunity and quality schools. K-12 school funding, higher education affordability, and whether education translates into sustainable housing security are concrete policy demands from a constituency where college degrees are the baseline expectation. A representative who treats education as settled policy rather than as an ongoing material concern of the majority will miss the district's primary economic anxiety." },
          { name: "Racial and Ethnic Coalition Maintenance Across Economic Equity", tag: "careful", why: "The district's demographic diversity (47% white, 26% Black, 15% Hispanic, 7% Asian, 6% AIAN) means that homeownership rates, wage equity, and wealth-building access vary significantly by race and ethnicity — and that constituencies evaluate whether the incumbent is delivering equitable outcomes, not simply equitable rhetoric. Building a durable coalition across this diversity requires demonstrating specific policy commitments to racial equity in housing access, wages, and economic mobility that go beyond acknowledgment. The unopposed 2024 result creates an opportunity to strengthen these relationships through active constituent engagement; neglecting them creates an opening for a challenger who will." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Electoral Invulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 general election does not indicate that HD-30 is a safe Democratic seat — it indicates the absence of a Republican challenger. In a district where 58% of residents are renters, cost-of-living anxiety is acute, and demographic diversity means that no single constituency is a stable majority, the absence of opposition in one cycle does not predict resistance to a credible challenger in the next one. Any candidate or incumbent who interprets unopposed status as permission to reduce constituent engagement on housing, education, and economic equity issues is making a strategic error." },
          { name: "Assuming College Attainment Automatically Translates to Partisan Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated voters, particularly when younger and renting, are not a captive Democratic constituency — they are voters who evaluate candidates on whether specific policy solutions address their economic reality. In HD-30, where college education is the baseline but housing costs remain a constraint on economic security, a representative who assumes college-educated renters will vote Democratic regardless of performance on housing and cost-of-living policy is ignoring the material gap between educational attainment and economic security that defines the constituency's actual lived experience." }
