          { name: "Treating the 49-51 Margin as a Stable Result", tag: "avoid", why: "A one-percentage-point Republican win in a district with no dominant demographic coalition and substantial working-class economic anxiety is not evidence of GOP strength in NC-HD-32 — it is evidence of extraordinary closeness and vulnerability to turnout shifts, candidate-specific appeal, or local issues. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though 51% represents a safe base, rather than a precarious plurality requiring constant constituent engagement, will find themselves vulnerable in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Running a Turnout-Only Campaign Within One Racial Community", tag: "avoid", why: "With Black at 40.89%, white at 44.46%, and Hispanic at 10.55%, no single racial community represents a sustainable electoral majority in NC-HD-32 — a candidate must build coalition across all groups. Any campaign that treats the Black plurality as a guaranteed Democratic base while conceding white working-class voters, or conversely treats white voters as inherently Republican, will fail to win the persuadable middle of renters, younger families, and economically anxious households across racial lines who are genuinely open to both candidates' appeals." }
