          { name: "Partisan Tribal Messaging to Affluent Suburbs", tag: "avoid", why: "NC-HD-35 is a constituency of college-educated professionals in an affluent suburb, not a partisan primary electorate — cultural messaging and tribal appeals that work in primary elections or safe seats will actively repel voters who are making pragmatic choices about economic management and quality-of-life governance. A candidate who leans heavily on cultural or partisan positioning rather than demonstrating competence on the issues that directly affect professional households' lives is conceding ground to a more practical opponent in a district where pragmatism is the dominant voting logic." },
          { name: "Taking the 27.1% Renter Constituency for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "One-quarter of the district rents rather than owns, and in an affluent suburb this population may well be young professionals, graduate students, or families in transition — not a Democratic base to be automatically assumed. These voters are likely evaluating housing affordability, economic opportunity, whether they can build assets in this community, and school quality if they have children. A candidate who defaults to assuming renters are Democratic voters or who does not engage their specific economic concerns is missing a constituency that in a 50-50 district has direct electoral weight and whose actual interests may not align with partisan stereotypes." }
