          { name: "Education & Higher Education Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 67.6% college attainment—among the highest in the state—and a $138K median income, NC-HD-40 is a professional-class district where education quality is both a personal priority and a household economic concern. Public school quality directly affects property values and family quality of life; higher education affordability shapes whether younger professionals remain in-state for workforce development. This constituency votes on these issues directly and holds representatives accountable for legislative outcomes on K-12 and university funding." },
          { name: "Healthcare System Quality & Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "A college-educated, high-income, 42.5-year-old median-age district with substantial professional employment in healthcare and adjacent sectors will evaluate healthcare policy and system performance with sophistication. Healthcare affordability, Medicaid/insurance access, and the viability of quality hospital and specialist services in the region are concrete constituent concerns, not abstract policy debates. Legislative record on healthcare expansion, insurance regulation, and provider network quality are measurable and will be scrutinized." },
          { name: "Cost-of-Living & Housing Affordability for Renters & Young Professionals", tag: "careful", why: "The 21.9% renter rate in a $138K median-income district signals that housing costs are rising faster than incomes for a material segment of the constituency—particularly younger professionals in early career phases. This community is not uniformly affluent; rental pressure is a real quality-of-life concern that Republican opponents will exploit through supply-side housing messaging. Any incumbent must address housing affordability specifically rather than treating it as a subordinate issue to homeowner concerns." },
          { name: "Tax Fairness & Regulatory Burden Critique", tag: "avoid", why: "The 38% Republican opposition in a professional-class district signals that business-oriented and ideologically conservative voters view the incumbent's tax and regulatory record as excessive. Republicans will deploy tax-fairness messaging, claims of regulatory overreach, and fiscal-management critique to close the 24-point gap; any incumbent who ignores this line of attack or dismisses it as ideological bad-faith is ceding persuadable swing voters. The 62% result is not bulletproof against a well-funded challenger who can credibly articulate a cost-of-doing-business or tax-burden message to business-community swing voters." },
          { name: "Partisan Culture War Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "Professional-class voters with high education and income levels typically prioritize substantive policy outcomes—schools, hospitals, tax dollars well-spent—over cultural grievance politics. An incumbent who leads with partisan culture-war messaging rather than concrete legislative results on education, healthcare, and fiscal management will alienate the swing segment of the district that evaluates on competence rather than tribal identity. This district is 62% Democratic by choice on education and healthcare grounds, not by reflexive partisan loyalty; messaging should reinforce that material foundation rather than substituting identity politics." }
