          "Develop a renter-focused housing affordability agenda: at 35.7%, renters represent more than one-third of the district and face material pressure at the $60,603 median income level; rent burden, tenant protections, and the link between housing stability and economic mobility are direct quality-of-life issues that engage this swing constituency on their lived reality rather than abstract property-tax ideology.",
          "Build workforce development and community college access into core legislative identity: at 26.4% college attainment, the district has substantial constituency for education and training investment; positioning education as instrumental to regional economic opportunity rather than as abstract social policy speaks to the working-age (37.4 median age) constituency evaluating whether government delivers on their material advancement.",
          "Treat the 57-43 result as a contested outcome, not a stable floor: the incumbent consolidated white homeowners but did not achieve majority support among Black residents or renters; any candidate who assumes the margin is durable rather than engaging these constituencies with specificity on their actual governance concerns will find the seat vulnerable to a challenger offering more authentic constituent partnership."
