          { name: "Healthcare Access & Affordability for Uninsured & Underinsured Population", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district with $37,287 median income and a significant renter majority, health insurance coverage and the affordability of medical care are direct household budget concerns, not abstract policy questions. Whether Medicaid has been expanded in North Carolina, whether the representative has fought for that expansion or against it, and what the path forward is on healthcare cost reduction are the practical issues that structure how this constituency evaluates representation. A candidate without a clear, achievable healthcare affordability plan will appear indifferent to the daily reality of households making trade-offs between medicine and rent." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity & Community-Specific Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The ~48% of the district's population not captured in the four reported Census categories represents a significant, potentially AIAN, multiracial, or immigrant constituency whose specific interests and governance expectations cannot be inferred from the Black, White, Hispanic, and Asian percentages reported. Without authentic engagement of these communities — understanding their particular economic concerns, healthcare access barriers, and whether they feel seen by current representation — a candidate risks governing a district while missing nearly half its population's actual needs and perspectives." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology over Material Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency that consolidated around 65% Democratic support did so because they evaluated that choice as materially beneficial to their economic survival — not because they are ideologically committed to Democratic abstraction. Any candidate, regardless of party, who campaigns on partisan principles rather than concrete economic plans to reduce housing costs, increase wages, improve healthcare affordability, or create stable employment will find themselves disconnected from a constituency that votes its material interest." },
          { name: "Assuming the 65% Margin is Structurally Durable", tag: "avoid", why: "In a working-poor district with significant renter majority and acute economic pressure, a 65-35 margin is a preference for current Democratic positioning, not a structural Democratic supermajority. If an incumbent fails to deliver on the housing affordability, wage, or healthcare promises that drove the 65%, or if a credible Republican challenger offers a specific alternative economic plan that appears more concrete and achievable, the margin compresses rapidly. The current result reflects current performance and messaging; it does not insulate against a challenger who better addresses the constituency's material concerns." }
