          { name: "Workforce Development & Community College Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 31% college attainment rate significantly below state average combined with $57,261 median income indicates a constituency where high school graduation is often the educational ceiling and stable employment typically requires post-secondary vocational or technical training. Community college affordability, employer partnerships, apprenticeship programs, and the practical pathways from secondary education to stable, wage-sustaining work are material governance issues with direct household impact. Demonstrable engagement with community colleges, unions, and employers on workforce development is a differentiating legislative priority." },
          { name: "Service-Sector Workforce Protections & Wage Equity", tag: "careful", why: "With a 50% renter, $57k-income, majority-minority composition, substantial portions of the constituency work in service-sector, hospitality, retail, and care work where wage theft, hour manipulation, and precarious employment are routine practices. Wage-and-hour enforcement, paid leave access, and protections against arbitrary scheduling are not abstract labor issues — they are direct household economics for workers whose income volatility makes month-to-month budgeting unstable. A representative must demonstrate specific legislative engagement with these issues, acknowledging that 'jobs' without wage protection and schedule stability do not function as economic security." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Election as Democratic Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 result reflects opposition candidate failure or low Republican resource commitment in a changing district — not demonstrated strength of constituency support or political invulnerability. In a district this economically constrained and demographically complex, economic anxiety is present and latent; a challenger who credibly identifies housing costs, wage stagnation, or service gaps and offers specific remedies can mobilize opposition even in a district that looks 'safe' in isolation. This is a vulnerability, not a victory." },
          { name: "Assuming Generic Minority Representation Satisfies Distinct Communities", tag: "avoid", why: "A 48% Black, 12.62% Hispanic, 7.51% Asian district requires representation grounded in specific understanding of each community's distinct economic and social concerns — not absorption into a generic 'diverse constituency' narrative. The Black community's healthcare access and employment discrimination issues, the Hispanic community's immigrant legal status and wage-theft vulnerabilities, and the Asian community's small-business development and discrimination concerns are not interchangeable; conflating them under a diversity umbrella signals lack of specific constituent engagement and leaves opening for opponents who do differentiate." }
