          { name: "Renter Population & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 31.3% renter rate is the second-highest in comparable competitive districts, and in a $67,884-income area, renters are likely facing acute housing-cost pressure in an inflationary environment. This population has not yet fully consolidated into the Republican margin—addressing housing affordability, rental-market stabilization, and economic opportunity for younger adults directly is both authentic constituent service and a strategic defense against challenger appeals to the 45% opposition. A candidate who engages renters credibly on their material concerns can prevent this population from becoming a swing constituency." },
          { name: "Demographic-Gap Population & Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "The 13.2% gap between the four reported Census categories and 100% likely represents multiracial residents, recent immigrants, and other communities whose voting behavior is not yet consolidated. This is a meaningful population—larger than typical margin thresholds—and their perception of whether state government is responsive to them will influence whether they vote, and for whom. Authentic constituent service and visible engagement with this population's concerns around economic mobility and healthcare access is both a governance obligation and a strategic necessity in a 55-45 district." },
          { name: "Treating 55% as a Secure Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 result is a solid lead but not a durable floor in a district where 31.3% of residents rent and 13.2% of the population falls outside the major Census categories—populations that together represent 44.5% of the constituency. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 55% represents a comfortable hold, rather than a margin won by alignment with the homeowner majority and indifference to renter and demographic-gap constituencies, is arithmetically and strategically wrong. This margin is defensible only through active constituent engagement across all three populations." },
          { name: "Assuming the Demographic-Gap Population Is Politically Inert", tag: "avoid", why: "A 13.2% demographic gap in any district is a population whose behavior cannot be assumed; in NC-HD-64, this group likely includes younger, more diverse, and recently arrived residents for whom traditional partisan loyalties may not be primary. Any candidate who treats this population as a residual category rather than as an active constituency requiring specific engagement risks allowing it to consolidate around a challenger's appeal on economic mobility, healthcare, or other material concerns. In a 55-45 district, this population is large enough to determine the outcome." }
