          { name: "Educational Equity & Higher Education Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 44.3% college-educated rate—substantially above state and national averages—signals a constituency with direct financial stakes in education funding, student debt management, and access to quality schools. This educated majority evaluates legislators on higher education investment, K-12 equity, and whether government is treating education as the foundation for opportunity. A legislator who leads on student debt relief, higher education funding, and equitable K-12 resource distribution speaks directly to the values and material interests of the district's demographic majority." },
          { name: "Racial Equity in Economic Opportunity & Criminal Justice", tag: "careful", why: "With 34.62% Black and 18.63% Hispanic residents, NC-HD-66 is a district where racial equity is not a boutique issue but a core constituent concern affecting more than half the population. These communities expect substantive legislative action on criminal justice reform, equitable hiring and contracting, and economic development access—not performative statements. A representative who delivers measurable outcomes on police accountability, sentencing reform, and targeted economic development for communities of color strengthens the coalition; one who treats racial equity as a secondary concern risks defection to a challenger perceived as more credible on these material issues." },
          { name: "Treating a 75% Margin as an Unshakeable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75-25 result in a college-educated, diverse, renter-majority district reflects real constituency consolidation, but it is not an unshakeable mandate. This demographic profile—educated, urban, younger, racially diverse—evaluates on delivery of specific policy outcomes: housing access, educational investment, healthcare equity, and criminal justice reform. Any representative who treats this 75% as permission to focus elsewhere or to assume loyalty based on partisan identity rather than material delivery will find this margin eroding if a credible challenger emerges who speaks to housing, education, and equity with greater specificity." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Hispanic Community or Treating It as Secondary to Black-White Dynamics", tag: "avoid", why: "The 18.63% Hispanic population represents nearly one-fifth of the district and is growing as a share of the overall population in North Carolina. Economic mobility, healthcare access, immigration policy impact, and equitable service delivery are the core concerns of this community; treating Hispanic constituents as an afterthought or absorbing them into a generic 'people of color' narrative rather than delivering specific service and advocacy is a strategic vulnerability. A challenger who demonstrates deep engagement with the Hispanic community's specific needs—workplace safety, pathway programs, healthcare translation—can peel off meaningful votes in a competitive cycle." }
