          "The 28.4% renter rate, combined with the 50.2% college attainment and $82,144 income, reveals a district with meaningful economic diversity within its educated, suburban profile: renters are younger professionals, graduate students, or service-sector workers whose economic security depends on housing affordability and wage growth rather than asset appreciation. The 71.75% white population, 12.21% Black, 8.24% Hispanic, and 4.1% Asian together account for 96.3% of reported demographics, with approximately 3.7% representing other communities — a highly integrated, economically diverse suburban constituency. Any candidate who campaigns this district as though it is a homogeneous Republican enclave is misreading both its economic composition and its demonstrated electoral behavior. The path to holding or winning HD-74 runs through persuading college-educated homeowners on local governance competence and reaching the renter constituency on economic mobility and housing affordability — not through base consolidation alone."
