          { name: "Property Tax Burden & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 74.3% homeownership (inverse of 25.7% renter rate) and $64,129 income, the district is a homeowner-heavy community where property tax increases directly reduce take-home pay and housing security. Property tax fairness, assessment practices, and tax relief for middle-income homeowners are the tangible governance issues that affect household budgets month-to-month. The 25.7% renter rate also signals housing affordability pressure among younger or lower-income residents who cannot access the homeownership market, making housing cost a cross-class concern that spans both renters and homeowners struggling with tax burdens." },
          { name: "Credibility of the 24% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "In a district where 76% voted for the incumbent, the 24% opposition is mathematically small but strategically important as a base for any challenger. The size and composition of that 24% — whether it is concentrated among renters, younger voters, or specific geographic areas within the district — is the primary intelligence gap. If the opposition is organized around specific economic grievances and has real infrastructure for reaching persuadable voters in the 76% majority, the margin may be more fragile than it appears; if it is scattered and disorganized, 76% is genuinely dominant. Any challenger must understand the opposition's actual composition and messaging before assuming the margin is stable." },
          { name: "Treating 76% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 76-24 split is numerically dominant but cannot be dismissed as electorally immovable in a district this economically stressed and this dependent on working-class household stability. If economic conditions deteriorate — healthcare costs spike, property taxes rise sharply, wages stagnate further — a significant margin like this can compress rapidly if an opposition candidate frames a credible alternative around material economic improvement. The assumption that 76% is secure because it is large enough ignores the actual fragility of working-class constituencies when their economic circumstances are under pressure." },
          { name: "Ideological or Abstract Policy Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with 21.6% college attainment and $64,129 median income is not persuaded by ideological arguments or abstract policy positioning; it responds to whether government is solving concrete, lived problems. Any candidate — incumbent or challenger — who leads with partisan rhetoric, conservative or progressive ideology, or policy proposals disconnected from material household concerns will fail to persuade this constituency. This is a district that evaluates on results and specific remedies, not on worldview or partisan identity." }
