          "Lead with the 61.4% renter, 32.2-year-old, college-educated constituency's actual economic concerns: housing cost as a percentage of income, student debt management, healthcare access and affordability, and whether entry-level professional work translates into economic mobility. These are the issues that determine whether a 57.9% college-educated constituency remains Democratic or becomes persuadable.",
          "Engage the district's genuine racial diversity—51.99% white, 23.45% Black, 17.86% Hispanic, 2.98% Asian—with specific constituent attention to each community's distinct economic and healthcare concerns rather than broad-brush minority outreach. In a district where no group represents a clear supermajority, authentic multicommunity engagement is both a governance obligation and an electoral prerequisite.",
          "Do not treat this district as safe because it is currently uncontested—treat it as a district where the Democratic incumbent has the advantage of incumbency and a naturally sympathetic constituency, but where genuine Republican attention to renter economics, housing policy, and cost-of-living governance can create real electoral competition. The 2024 unopposed result is noise; the demographic and economic fundamentals are the signal."
