          "The 78% Republican result appears to rest on a solid foundation of white, working-class, homeowner voters for whom Republican fiscal conservatism and cultural messaging resonate. However, at a median age of 44.5 and median income of $54,982, this constituency is not primarily affluent — it is older, financially constrained, and increasingly vulnerable to healthcare costs, property tax increases, and the economic disruption of deindustrialization or service-sector decline. The Hispanic population of roughly 11.15% in a district this economically stressed represents both a demographic reality and a potential electoral swing constituency if economic anxiety becomes acute. The incumbent's 78% margin creates a temptation to assume stability, but that margin is built on delivery to a specific economic constituency: working-class households whose material concerns are healthcare affordability, local job availability, cost of living, and whether government is keeping taxes and regulations from crushing their household finances. A representative who loses touch with these material economic concerns — or who becomes identified with policies that are perceived as benefiting the wealthy while costs rise for working-class families — will find that the 78% margin is far more vulnerable than the raw number suggests."
