          "Prioritize service delivery to the 57% Black and Hispanic demographic majority on the issues that matter to working and professional families in those communities: K-12 education quality and funding equity, healthcare access and affordability (particularly maternal and preventive care), economic mobility pathways, and criminal justice reform — these are the material concerns that hold a coalition together when partisan assumptions are not sufficient.",
          "Engage the 40.8% renter constituency with specificity on housing affordability, tenant protections, and the distinction between the needs of early-career renters and long-term rent-burdened households — a $92,759 median income district with 40.8% renters indicates a two-tier constituency where housing cost burden is a real economic issue for a material portion of the population.",
          "Monitor the White 29.1% and Asian 8.12% communities for signs of coalition tension: particularly around education access, healthcare equity, and whether these communities perceive themselves as represented or marginalized — in a 57% communities-of-color district, the minority coalition partner's satisfaction is essential to preventing primary challenges or third-party disruption."
