          { name: "Healthcare Cost Sustainability for Affluent Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $120,367 median income with 60.1% college attainment, HD-98 residents are affluent enough to have comprehensive health insurance, yet high enough income to bear significant healthcare cost increases year-over-year. The Democratic 52% suggests healthcare policy is a mobilizing concern for educated professionals—whether employer coverage costs, prescription drug pricing, or the viability of healthcare in retirement. Any candidate holding or challenging this seat must address healthcare economics with specificity to affluent, insured households rather than treating healthcare as a poverty issue; the margin may turn on whether voters believe government policy is addressing their actual material concern." },
          { name: "Climate & Environmental Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "College-educated professionals at $120k+ income are the demographic cohort most likely to prioritize climate and environmental policy in their voting calculus—and the Democratic 52% in a district with these credentials suggests environmental governance may be a significant mobilizing issue. For Democratic incumbents, articulating a concrete record on environmental regulation, clean energy, and land use is essential; for Republican challengers, addressing this constituency's environmental concerns directly—rather than dismissing them—is necessary to prevent educated professionals from defaulting to Democratic positions based on perceived indifference." },
          { name: "Tax Policy & Regulatory Burden Among Affluent Professionals", tag: "careful", why: "The Republican 48% in a high-income, highly-educated district suggests meaningful professional support for lower tax burdens and reduced regulatory complexity—concerns that affluent professionals and small business owners legitimately hold regardless of education level. A Democratic incumbent must address why progressive tax policy serves educated professionals' interests; a Republican challenger must avoid rhetorical dismissal of educated voters' tax concerns as greed, instead framing tax relief as enabling professional investment and economic dynamism. The 52-48 margin suggests this issue is genuinely contested and could swing the seat if either candidate is perceived as indifferent to the other side's legitimate concern." },
          { name: "Treating Education Level as Determinative of Partisan Behavior", tag: "avoid", why: "While 60.1% college attainment is high, it does not make the district an automatic Democratic constituency—the 48% Republican support demonstrates that affluent, educated professionals hold diverse political values and evaluate based on concrete policy, not demographic destiny. Any candidate who campaigns to highly educated voters on the assumption that education automatically produces support for progressive positions risks appearing condescending and missing the opportunity to engage the actual policy concerns that drive this constituency's voting behavior." },
          { name: "Assuming 52% Represents a Stable Democratic Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A two-point margin in a district this affluent and educated is not a comfortable Democratic floor—it is a narrow plurality in a genuinely contested seat where Republican strength (48%) is built on educated, professional, affluent voters who are actively evaluating their vote. Any Democratic incumbent who treats 52% as a stable base and fails to actively defend against Republican outreach to educated professionals, or any Republican challenger who assumes the 48% is a ceiling rather than a mobilizable coalition, will fail to recognize this seat's true competitive nature and miss the opportunity to expand their coalition within the educated, affluent constituency." }
