          "The demographic composition — 67.86% white, 10.75% Black, 9.57% Asian, 6.7% Hispanic — suggests a diverse urban professional community where racial and ethnic diversity is higher than the state average, but where the dominant constituency is white-collar professionals and entrepreneurs with direct economic stakes in property values, municipal services, business regulation, and tax policy. The 27.8% renter rate indicates a meaningful subset of younger professionals or those in transition, but homeownership is clearly the norm, and homeowners in this income bracket are evaluating local governance on schools, infrastructure maintenance, public safety, and whether tax dollars are being spent efficiently. Any candidate seeking to hold or win this seat must demonstrate specific competence on the issues that matter to $120,000-income homeowners: municipal fiscal management, infrastructure maintenance, development policy that protects property values, and school system performance. Generic appeals to partisan identity or ideological positioning will underperform in a district where voters are evaluating on outcomes, not team loyalty."
