          { name: "Racial and Ethnic Coalition Building Across Four Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "With no racial group exceeding 56% of the population and 55.6% white, 14.21% Hispanic/Latino, 12.66% Black, and 11.85% Asian residents, NC-HD-105 is a genuinely multi-racial district where a 50-50 partisan result suggests the incumbent's coalition does not hold automatic support from minority communities. Each of these groups — particularly Hispanic/Latino and Black constituents — likely contains voters whose support is contingent on whether governance responds to their distinct economic priorities: wage access, small business support, healthcare equity, and inclusive housing opportunity. Building durable support requires specific constituent work with each community, not aggregated minority outreach." },
          { name: "Understanding the Composition and Motivations of 50% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "A 50-50 tie in a high-income, college-educated, racially diverse district is not ideologically predetermined — it reflects genuine constituency disagreement about governance priorities and whether current leadership serves everyone or protects established interests. Before any strategic decision, a candidate must diagnose whether the opposition 50% is concentrated among renters frustrated by housing costs, among younger professionals evaluating on economic mobility, among racial/ethnic minorities feeling governance is not responsive to their needs, or across multiple groups simultaneously. The path to 51% depends on this diagnostic work; without it, strategy is guesswork against half a constituency." },
          { name: "Framing High Income as Evidence of Inclusive Governance Success", tag: "avoid", why: "The $100,307 median household income masks material internal stratification: 45% of households do not own homes, likely indicating younger professionals and service workers whose real wages have not kept pace with housing costs despite high household income totals. Any candidate who frames the district's prosperity as evidence that current governance is working for everyone will be directly contradicted by the lived experience of renters and service workers in a high-cost-of-living environment, and will lose credibility with the 50% opposition who experience prosperity differently than homeowners do. This framing is both inaccurate and electorally dangerous in a 50-50 district." },
          { name: "Treating the Incumbent's 50% as a Stable Floor Rather Than a Contested Plurality", tag: "avoid", why: "A 50% result is by definition not a majority — it is one option in a two-option choice among genuinely divided voters. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 50% represents a secure coalition is misreading the fundamental strategic fact about the district: there is an equally large opposition constituency whose needs are not currently being met by current governance. This is a seat where the incumbent must actively earn continued support from swing voters and opposition constituencies, not one where existing coalition consolidation is sufficient to hold the seat." }
