          "The racial and ethnic diversity of NC-HD-105 — 55.6% white, 14.21% Hispanic/Latino, 12.66% Black, 11.85% Asian, with roughly 5.7% unaccounted for in the reported categories — creates a fourth dimension of this complexity. In a district where no single racial group comprises more than 56% of the population, a 50-50 partisan split suggests that the incumbent's path to 50% does not depend on consolidating a racial or ethnic supermajority, but rather on building a coalition that spans racial lines around shared economic and governance concerns. The 38.4-year median age — slightly below the state median — indicates a constituency with both established professionals and younger adults trying to build wealth in a high-cost-of-living environment. Any candidate who wants to hold or expand beyond 50% must diagnose specifically whether the opposition 50% is concentrated among renters frustrated by housing costs, among racial/ethnic minorities feeling governance is not responsive to their priorities, among younger professionals with debt and housing concerns, or across all these groups. The path to 51% depends on understanding what the 50% opposition actually wants and delivering material answers."
