          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 46.5% renters with $68,879 median household income, nearly half the district's households are paying a significant share of income toward rent in an urban market where housing costs have risen faster than wages. Rental protections, affordable housing production, zoning reform that increases supply, and tenant-landlord regulations are not abstract policy issues — they are direct material concerns for the constituency that voted 100% for the incumbent in 2024. A representative with a concrete legislative record on housing affordability demonstrates accountability to this largest structural demographic in the district and builds durability against future opposition." },
          { name: "Public Education Funding and College Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 40.9% college-attainment rate is substantially above North Carolina median and signals a college-educated constituency with direct stakes in school quality and the affordability of higher education. Families at this educational level evaluate representatives on whether state education funding is rising or declining, whether university systems are accessible to middle-income students, and whether the public school system is delivering college-preparatory opportunity. This is a measurable constituency concern with policy levers the state legislature controls directly." },
          { name: "Economic Opportunity for Younger Residents", tag: "careful", why: "The 34.7-year median age combined with 46.5% renter rate means a substantial portion of the district is younger adults not yet settled into homeownership, evaluating whether they can afford to stay in the community long-term. The $68,879 median income is respectable but not sufficient for housing affordability in many urban North Carolina markets, creating a tension between the district's college-attainment and the economic mobility that younger residents actually experience. If younger residents perceive limited opportunity, this constituency has the capacity to organize alternative representation even in a district that voted 100% one cycle prior." },
          { name: "Treating 100% Unopposed Result as Permanent Coalition Lock", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100-0 result reflects incumbent strength and aligned voting in a single cycle, not an immutable coalition structure. The 57% Black, college-educated, renter-majority, younger-median-age constituency has potentially divergent material interests — homeowners versus renters, older versus younger professionals, education-focused families versus service workers — that an unopposed candidate may not have had to reconcile. Any representative who interprets 100% as permission to neglect constituent service or legislative agenda on housing and opportunity issues risks finding that percentage shift sharply when opposition reemerges." },
          { name: "Overestimating Racial Solidarity Without Address to Material Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The 57% Black constituency did not vote 100% because racial identity alone determines voting behavior — it voted that way because the incumbent's alignment with the district's material concerns (housing, schools, opportunity) produced consensus. Over-relying on racial solidarity framing while under-delivering on housing affordability, education funding, or economic mobility for younger residents is a strategic error that assumes identity politics is sufficient in a college-educated constituency that evaluates on policy substance. The college-attainment rate and renter demographics both signal a constituency that will switch if material concerns are neglected." }
