          { name: "Older Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 47.6 median age, 79.3% homeownership, and $55,228 income define a constituency navigating the working-to-fixed-income transition, where property tax increases, healthcare cost inflation, and Medicare sustainability are direct household economic concerns. At this income level and age, the difference between a legislator who addresses property tax fairness and one who does not is the difference between a durable 67% and a contested 55%. This is the material foundation of the district's Republican base, and active legislative attention to these issues is the cost of holding it." },
          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access & Hospital Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "A district with 47.6 median age and modest $55,228 income has meaningful healthcare access concerns, particularly around rural hospital viability and emergency services in smaller communities. Medicare recipients and older adults depend on local healthcare infrastructure, and legislative advocacy for rural hospital funding, telehealth access, and emergency services sustainability speaks directly to the district's economic security. A representative who engages these issues with specificity commands credibility with the core constituency." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap & Minority Constituent Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The ~4.6% population gap and the Hispanic/Latino share (implied but not isolated in the data) represent a small but real minority constituency in a 78.68% white district. In a race this district could theoretically lose through a combination of older white homeowner defection on economic grounds and mobilized minority turnout, treating this as a negligible constituency is arithmetically risky. Specific constituent service and legislative attention to economic mobility for minority communities builds baseline relationship that matters in close cycles." },
          { name: "Treating 67% as a License to Disengage from Older Homeowners' Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67% result in a district of older homeowners with $55,228 income is not a permanent mandate — it is a conditional majority held by alignment between the incumbent's legislative record and the constituency's economic security needs. The moment a representative stops delivering on property tax or healthcare cost management, or appears indifferent to rural service decline, the margin can collapse rapidly in a constituency that evaluates on whether government is protecting their economic interests." },
          { name: "Campaign Strategy Focused on Partisan Turnout Rather Than Economic Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "A district this old, this homeowner-heavy, and this modestly positioned economically will evaluate candidates first on whether they are addressing their material economic concerns — healthcare costs, property taxes, rural services — not on abstract partisan messaging. A campaign that relies on Republican party loyalty and GOTV intensity rather than substantive economic engagement ignores the district's actual decision-making criteria and leaves the seat vulnerable to an opposition candidate who campaigns on concrete economic relief." }
