          "The district's economic profile is the strategic battleground. At $54,428 median household income with 39.1% of households renting, NC-SD-4's material concern is not ideological alignment but whether their state legislator delivers concrete improvements in housing affordability, healthcare access, job quality, and public services. The 38.8 median age indicates a working-age constituency that will evaluate performance on these dimensions differently than older, property-owning constituencies elsewhere in the state. Black voters at 33.89% — the constituency with the lowest median income and highest renter concentration — will be the decisive swing constituency if they receive direct attention from a challenger on housing policy, Medicaid access, education funding, and job creation. Any incumbent who assumes this plurality will remain Democratic without engaging them on these issues, and any challenger who offers specific policy remedies to Black renters' economic security, Hispanic workers' job access, and the broader working-class constituency's material concerns, can shift the margin substantially from Newton's 55%."
