          { name: "College-Educated Professional Base Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 62% college-attainment rate and $89,962 income, combined with the 33-year median age, identify a constituency of educated professionals whose 66% Democratic vote reflects alignment with policies around quality-of-life, transit/infrastructure investment, schools, and economic opportunity in knowledge sectors. This is the base constituency that delivered the margin; losing pieces of this coalition through perceived inattention to urban amenities, schools, or economic development policy creates direct electoral risk. The incumbent must maintain active delivery on the walkable-neighborhood development, transit infrastructure, and educational investment priorities that make this coalition durable." },
          { name: "Multi-Racial Coalition Cohesion & Specific Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "With 67% white, 13% Black, 9.9% Hispanic/Latino, and 5% Asian residents, the district is meaningfully diverse and the 66% Democratic vote reflects multi-racial coalition building rather than white-collar professionals alone. This coalition is real but not automatic—economic divergences (renters vs. homeowners, service workers vs. professionals, newer immigrants vs. established residents) can fracture along demographic and class lines if specific communities perceive the incumbent as serving the median-income professional class over their material concerns. Black, Hispanic/Latino, and Asian communities require active constituent engagement around workforce development, healthcare access, business development support, and school equity; passive assumption of demographic loyalty without material delivery creates vulnerability to challenger appeals." },
          { name: "Treating the 66% Margin as Permission to Govern Without Active Coalition Maintenance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result is a substantial victory, but in a district with 50.5% renters, meaningful racial diversity, and significant economic inequality masked by median-income statistics, the coalition is held together by perceived delivery on specific material concerns rather than inherited partisan loyalty. Any incumbent who treats the 66% as a durable floor and reduces active constituent engagement, coalition maintenance, and specific economic-security messaging to renters and working families risks allowing a credible challenger to peel off 5-10 percentage points among renters or minority communities who conclude government is not serving them. This margin is durable only through continued active work." },
          { name: "Positioning as Aligned with Wealthy Homeowners Over Renters & Working Families", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 62%-college-educated district with $89,962 median income but 50.5% renter rate, challenger messaging that positions the incumbent as aligned with property-owner interests, development-industry priorities, and wealthy homeowners over renters and working families has direct electoral power. If the incumbent's record shows zoning or development decisions that increase housing scarcity and prices, insufficient tenant protections, or policies perceived as benefiting landlords over renters, a challenger can organize renters and working-class voters around economic-justice framing that erodes the Democratic coalition. The incumbent must maintain a visible record of rental-affordability advocacy and tenant-protection commitment to inoculate against this vulnerability." }
