          { name: "High-Income Urban Constituency Economic Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $129,563 median income and 71.1% college educated, NC-SD-16 is a constituency of affluent professionals and knowledge-sector workers sensitive to tax policy, regulatory burden on businesses, and the costs of living in high-income urban markets. A Democratic incumbent must demonstrate that party positions on taxation, business regulation, and zoning reform actually serve this constituency's economic interests — not impose costs on them — or risk persuasion challenges from Republicans offering alternative framings on deregulation and housing supply that appeal to affluent urban voters frustrated by affordability and complexity." },
          { name: "Asian Population Engagement & Electoral Volatility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 24.58% Asian population is the second-largest demographic group in the district and represents a politically volatile constituency that has shifted significantly in recent cycles across similar high-income urban districts. This community is not monolithic: different Asian ethnic groups respond to different policy appeals around education, economic mobility, immigration, and representation. Building a durable Democratic advantage requires specific engagement with distinct Asian communities and attention to their distinct policy concerns — not treating 'Asian voters' as a unified Democratic bloc." },
          { name: "Renter-Majority Constituency & Housing Policy", tag: "careful", why: "At 39.6% renter rate in a $129,563-income district, this is a constituency of young professionals and graduate students who are economically mobile but not yet homeowners — and whose housing concerns can be framed by either party. Democrats can lead on tenant protections and affordability, but Republicans can compete by offering zoning reform and housing supply deregulation as solutions to the same affordability problem. The renter-majority composition makes housing and affordability a primary swing issue, not a Democratic stronghold." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Results as Evidence of Invulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% Democratic result with no Republican challenger tells us only that the Republican Party organization failed to field a candidate — not that NC-SD-16 is a 65-35 Democratic seat or a Democratic stronghold. In a high-income, highly educated urban district, a well-resourced Republican campaign with sophisticated messaging on tax policy, regulation, or housing supply could plausibly contest a seat that appears unbeatable only because it went uncontested in 2024. Incumbent complacency is the primary risk to holding this seat." },
          { name: "Assuming Renter & Young Professional Constituencies Are Inherently Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "Renters and young professionals evaluate on economic mobility, housing access, and whether government is actually delivering material improvement in their lives — not on inherited partisan loyalty. A Republican candidate who frames zoning reform and housing supply deregulation as solutions to affordability, or who emphasizes business-friendly tax policy and regulatory efficiency, can appeal to this constituency if the Democratic incumbent is perceived as offering regulation without results. The 39.6% renter rate is a potential vulnerability, not a Democratic asset, if Democrats cannot deliver on housing affordability." }
