          { name: "Property Tax Fairness & Homeowner Economic Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 81.8% homeownership and $119,289 median income, property ownership is the primary wealth-building asset for the vast majority of this constituency; property tax policy is not a partisan issue but a direct household budget concern. Whether taxes are rising faster than incomes, whether assessment practices are predictable and fair, and whether state policy is protecting or eroding the economic value of residential property are questions this constituency evaluates with precision. A representative who delivers on property tax fairness, even within existing revenue constraints, builds durable support; one who is perceived as indifferent to homeowner cost pressures creates an opening for opposition on what should be consolidated ground." },
          { name: "Minority Community Economic Integration & Professional-Class Access", tag: "careful", why: "Asian (7.36%), Black (10.4%), and Hispanic (8.93%) communities together represent 26% of NC-SD-17, and in an affluent, professional-class district these communities include both immigrant professionals and families navigating barriers to economic integration in high-cost communities. Healthcare access, housing affordability for working professionals, educational equity, and whether economic opportunity is genuinely open to non-white professionals are the material governance concerns for these constituencies. A representative who treats them as separate 'minority outreach' rather than as constituent bases with distinct economic interests in a professional-class district risks both underestimating their electoral weight and missing the specificity of their governance needs." },
          { name: "Treating the 26% Opposition as Ideologically Fixed", tag: "avoid", why: "In an affluent district with 57.8% college attainment and significant homeownership, the 26% Republican support likely includes both ideological conservatives and affluent fiscal moderates who might be reachable if a Democratic candidate demonstrates competent stewardship of property values, tax fairness, and fiscal management. Writing off this constituency as irretrievable or campaigning only to consolidate the 74% assumes that the opposition's support is identity-based rather than interest-based — an assumption that is weaker in affluent professional-class districts where voters often prioritize competence and results over party loyalty. A candidate who does not test whether fiscal conservatism or local governance quality concerns create real persuasion opportunities leaves a 26% opening larger than it needs to be." },
          { name: "Neglecting Environmental Quality as a Property Value & Livability Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with $119,289 median income, high homeownership, and strong Democratic alignment, environmental protection is not primarily an ideological issue — it is a livability and property value protection issue for a constituency whose wealth is tied to residential desirability. Water quality, air quality, greenspace preservation, and land use that protects the character of established neighborhoods are the environmental issues that matter most to this constituency. A representative who allows environmental protection to become abstract partisan positioning, or who is perceived as indifferent to environmental quality concerns that directly affect property values and neighborhood character, cedes an issue that should be consolidated ground to any opposition challenger who highlights it." }
