          { name: "Positioning Lower-Income Constituents as Dependent Rather Than Economically Productive", tag: "avoid", why: "Framing renters, Hispanic workers, and lower-income Black constituents as dependent beneficiaries of government rather than as productive workers and taxpayers whose economic constraints are genuine governance problems will actively alienate a constituency that represents one-third of the district and is evaluating whether the incumbent understands their material reality. These constituencies respond to messaging that positions them as stakeholders in shared economic growth and as people whose productivity is being constrained by policy failures (housing costs, healthcare costs, wage stagnation) — not as populations requiring benevolence or charity." }
