          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40.4% renter rate, NC-SD-27 has a larger renter constituency than the national average, and renters at $67,381 median household income face real housing cost burden—particularly Black and Latino renters, who face both economic pressure and discrimination in housing markets. Tenant protection, anti-discrimination enforcement, affordable housing development, and rent stabilization are not abstract policy positions in this district; they are material concerns for nearly half the constituency. A Democratic incumbent who can demonstrate concrete delivery on housing—landlord accountability, enforcement of fair housing law, public investment in affordable units—transforms housing from a general partisan advantage into a specific, credible reason to choose this representative." },
          { name: "Education Quality and Funding for Majority-Minority Schools", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 30% Black population, 9.89% Hispanic, and 7.13% Asian residents, NC-SD-27 is a majority-minority district where school funding and performance directly affect the families building their futures here. The 37.5% college attainment rate indicates education is a ladder in this district, not a given, and younger families (38.9 median age) are actively evaluating school quality as they make housing and residency decisions. Democratic control at 61-39 means education is a Democratic strength, but only if that party delivers measurable improvement in school funding, teacher recruitment and retention, and equitable outcomes across racial lines—not just rhetoric about education's importance." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap Identity and Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four standard Census categories account for 95.55% of NC-SD-27's population, leaving a 4.45% gap of roughly 2,000-2,500 residents whose demographic identity is unspecified—likely American Indian, multiracial, or other communities not consistently reported. In a district where the 61% Democratic result is built on an explicit coalition of Black, Hispanic, and Asian voters, any population not accounted for in that coalition narrative risks being overlooked by both parties' candidate outreach. Identifying who comprises this gap and whether they have specific governance needs—tribal sovereignty if AIAN, mixed-race identity representation if multiracial—is a secondary but real strategic consideration for an incumbent seeking to strengthen rather than merely maintain current margins." },
          { name: "Economic Populism or Anti-Business Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "At $67,381 median household income with 37.5% college attainment, NC-SD-27 is a working-to-professional-class district with meaningful business ownership and professional employment alongside service and manufacturing jobs. Messaging that positions all business as exploitative or that defaults to anti-corporate rhetoric without specificity risks alienating small-business owners, independent professionals, and educated voters who understand that job creation and economic dynamism require functioning markets and entrepreneurship. The 61-39 Democratic margin is strong enough that a Democratic candidate can afford specificity on which business practices harm consumers or workers—predatory lending, wage theft, anti-competitive consolidation—without lapsing into generalized anti-business positioning that alienates legitimate economic actors in the district." },
          { name: "Assuming the 61% Margin Is Durable Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "While 61-39 is a solid Democratic advantage, it is not a structural majority in a state trending Republican, and it reflects Garrett's personal performance and constituent relationships as much as underlying district lean. A Democratic successor who campaigns as though 61% is a floor rather than an achievement—who neglects housing, education, and healthcare delivery in favor of abstract ideology—risks finding that the 39% opposition mobilizes more effectively or that the 47% voters-of-color coalition experiences lower turnout or shifted preferences if they perceive the incumbent as not delivering. This seat requires active, specific constituent engagement on the issues that matter to renters, students, and families of color—not the quiet confidence of a safe Democratic district." }
