          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security and Property Tax Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 71.3% homeownership, $56,640 median income, and 41.6 median age, NC-SD-29 is a community of older homeowners whose net worth is heavily concentrated in property equity and whose economic stability depends on whether home values hold and property tax burdens remain manageable. Legislative credibility on property tax fairness, transparency in assessments, and protection of home equity is the most direct way to maintain alignment with a constituency whose material concerns are fundamentally about whether they can sustain the homeownership status that distinguishes them from the 28.7% renter population." },
          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Engagement and Economic Representation", tag: "careful", why: "At 18.46% Black and 11.11% Hispanic, these communities represent 29.57% of the district's population — nearly one-third — yet their concentration in the 31% opposition suggests limited alignment with the incumbent's current message or positioning. Whether this reflects deliberate policy disagreement, genuine under-mobilization of their constituent concerns around workforce access and economic mobility, or messaging failure is not immediately clear from the electoral result alone; however, in a district where the margin is 69-31, any shift of 5-7 percentage points in minority community mobilization or turnout is sufficient to materially change the outcome. These constituencies deserve specific legislative attention, not absorption into the homeowner-majority narrative." },
          { name: "Treating 69% as an Immobile Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69% result in a district with 29.57% non-white population, $56,640 median income, and 28.7% renters is a reflection of current alignment between the incumbent and the homeowner majority, not a structural ceiling that cannot be moved by a credible challenger who mobilizes the opposition's actual concerns. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 69% is immobile rather than contingent on maintaining credibility with working-class homeowners while actively engaging the Black, Hispanic, and renter constituencies is misreading the district's actual political composition." },
          { name: "Positioning the District as Culturally Conservative Rather Than Economically Focused", tag: "avoid", why: "The absence of a significant college-educated class and university institutions suggests that cultural debates — DEI, curriculum wars, sexual orientation — may be less salient to most constituents than practical questions about whether regional employers are stable, whether wages are keeping pace with inflation, and whether homeownership remains financially sustainable. A representative or challenger who leads with abstract cultural conservatism rather than demonstrable economic competence and constituent service is missing the material foundation of this district's actual politics and may activate opposition unnecessarily among constituencies that might otherwise remain aligned." }
