          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Economy", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 38.9% renter rate with $68,359 median household income, a significant portion of NC-SD-32's constituency faces direct housing cost burden and economic insecurity despite college education and professional employment. Rental market dynamics, tenant protections, affordable housing supply, and the tension between development and affordability are not abstract policy issues but material constituent concerns. A legislative agenda anchored in renter-community economic security — rather than treating housing as a developer or investor issue — aligns Democratic governance with the lived experience of more than one-third of the district and demonstrates responsiveness to a demographic that is often taken for granted." },
          { name: "Black & Hispanic Community Economic Opportunity & Equity", tag: "lean-into", why: "Black residents (23.03%) and Hispanic residents (12.39%) together comprise 35.42% of the district — a substantial plurality whose economic mobility, workforce access, criminal justice experiences, and community investment are core constituent concerns distinct from the college-educated professional majority. The 69% Democratic result reflects these communities' support, but that support is not infinite and requires sustained, specific engagement on their own priorities rather than absorption into broad Democratic messaging. Sustained investment in community-rooted constituent service, economic development partnerships with minority-owned businesses, and accountability on policing and equity is both substantive governance and coalition maintenance that prevents erosion to 62% or worse." },
          { name: "Public Safety & Competent Governance Perception", tag: "careful", why: "The 31% Republican opposition in a district this Democratic likely concentrates among business owners, older homeowners, and working-class voters skeptical of Democratic governance's capacity to maintain public order and fiscal discipline. This constituency is not winnable through partisan persuasion, but it can be neutralized through demonstrated competence on public safety, fiscal management, and economic growth — showing that Democratic priorities can coexist with safe communities undermines the most credible challenger argument. Neglecting this perception or ceding the 'governance competence' frame to Republicans risks allowing a credible challenger to activate latent concern and shift the result from 69% toward the high 50s." },
          { name: "Treating 69% as a Permanent Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69% result in a swing state is strong but not unassailable, and the coalition's internal diversity — college-educated professionals, younger renters, Black and Hispanic communities, and urban-oriented service workers — requires sustained, differentiated engagement to maintain. Defaulting to broad Democratic messaging, treating any constituency segment as safe, or assuming that the incumbent's personal popularity is sufficient to hold the seat across multiple election cycles invites a credible challenger to build strength among neglected communities. The margin is substantial enough to absorb modest erosion, but sustained complacency or inattention to coalition maintenance is how 69% becomes 62% or worse." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization Over Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "NC-SD-32's Democratic alignment is fundamentally about education, economic mobility, and professional employment — not inherited partisan loyalty or ideological consistency. A legislative agenda that prioritizes partisan messaging or ideological symbolism over tangible delivery on housing, jobs, education, and fiscal management risks alienating the college-educated, working-class, and minority constituents whose material concerns are concrete. In a swing state, the most credible challenger is often one who positions as pragmatic and focused on constituent delivery rather than partisan conflict — allowing that frame to go unchallenged is a strategic gift to opposition." }
