          "This is a district that delivered a 42-point margin to the Republican incumbent in 2024, yet the underlying economic profile suggests vulnerability to pocketbook messaging, not partisan identity. Median household income of $62,738 means household budgets are tight, and a 28% renter rate compounds housing stress. The electorate is not college-educated (21%), not particularly young (median age 40.5), and appears motivated by tangible economic security rather than cultural or ideological appeals. The winning formula here is not about turning out true believers—it is about demonstrating that a candidate will reduce the cost of living, protect wages, and create pathways to stable work. A challenger or vulnerable incumbent who can credibly speak to these bread-and-butter concerns while respecting the district's socially conservative preferences will find an opening.",
