          { name: "Housing Affordability & Tenant Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 57.5% renters — substantially above typical baselines — and a median household income of $71,260 in what is likely an urban or inner-suburban market, housing costs are a material economic issue for the majority of constituents. Rent control, tenant protection, eviction prevention, and housing development policy are direct quality-of-life concerns for a renter-majority constituency. A legislator who delivers specific policy wins on housing affordability and tenant rights consolidates the high-propensity young adult and minority voter base; one who is silent on this issue leaves constituents available to a challenger with concrete housing policy commitments." },
          { name: "Demographic Coalition Durability Among Young Adults & Minorities", tag: "careful", why: "The 59.44% minority population (43.31% Black, 16.13% Hispanic) and 33.4-year median age represent a coalition that is not inherited — it must be earned through demonstrated economic opportunity and responsive governance. Hispanic voters in particular have demonstrated swing capacity in recent elections, and a culturally conservative Hispanic candidate with grassroots support could credibly challenge an incumbent perceived as taking minority voters for granted. Similarly, young adults evaluate on whether government is delivering on education, housing, and economic mobility — not partisan registration. An unopposed 2024 result offers no proof that this coalition is consolidated; the next cycle will require active engagement on the material concerns that hold it together." },
          { name: "Running as a Partisan Democrat Without Local Governance Credibility", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% unopposed election produced no demonstrated record of legislative effectiveness, constituent problem-solving, or local governance achievement. Entering the next cycle with partisan messaging alone — rather than a specific legislative record and constituent service track record — leaves the incumbent vulnerable to a challenger who can point to specific local failures or superior constituent responsiveness. In a safe Democratic district, this vulnerability is not existential, but it is real and exploitable by a credible primary or independent challenger." },
          { name: "Assuming 100% Democratic Registration is Equivalent to Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed win in 2024 is not evidence of electoral durability in a competitive cycle. A district with 57.5% renters, a median age of 33.4, and a 59.44% minority population is capable of mobilizing around a challenger if the incumbent is perceived as invisible, ineffective, or unresponsive to material concerns. Without visible constituent service infrastructure, legislative achievements, and authentic engagement with the district's economic and social priorities, the incumbent is at risk of being outworked by a credible challenger in a primary or of facing a meaningful independent challenge in a general election. Partisan registration is a floor, not a ceiling, in this analysis." }
