          { name: "Education Quality & Public Investment in a College-Educated District", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 56.9% college-educated population signals a constituency invested in education as both personal aspiration and community good—meaning K-12 public school quality, teacher compensation, and equitable education funding are direct material concerns, not abstract policy positions. In a district where education has tangibly shaped individual economic trajectories, voters evaluate aggressively on whether public schools receive adequate investment and whether governance delivers results. A representative who can articulate specific, evidence-based approaches to education quality and takes the K-12 sector seriously as a core constituency concern (not subordinate to higher-ed or generic school-choice rhetoric) better reflects what this district's 56.9% college attainment actually means about its priorities." },
          { name: "Diverse Coalition Maintenance Across Black & Hispanic Communities", tag: "careful", why: "Black (23.42%) and Hispanic (18.06%) residents together comprise 41.48% of the district—a substantial coalition partner whose electoral participation and candidate preference are necessary to any durable majority. In unopposed elections, incumbent attention to minority community priorities can fade relative to periods of active competition; the 2024 unopposed result may have masked latent dissatisfaction around economic opportunity, representation in appointed boards and civic institutions, or specific policy gaps. Any incumbent or challenger who treats these communities as reliably Democratic and assumes low-effort engagement will underestimate both their political leverage and their capacity to mobilize alternative candidates if they conclude governance is not serving them." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology Posturing in a Pragmatically Oriented District", tag: "avoid", why: "A district where 56.9% hold college degrees is substantially composed of voters who evaluate candidates primarily on demonstrated competence, specific policy understanding, and tangible results—not on partisan tribal affiliation or ideological purity. Any candidate who leads with abstract conservative or progressive rhetoric, rather than grounding positions in evidence and specific application to the material concerns of educated renters and young professionals, will underperform with a constituency that has demonstrated it expects sophisticated governance." },
          { name: "Taking the Unopposed Result as Evidence of Long-Term Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election reflects a specific moment in opposition party strategy and resource allocation; it does not establish that the incumbent has built a durable, expansion-resistant coalition. In a district with substantial renter populations, younger median age, and diverse racial composition, latent dissatisfaction around housing costs, education quality, or specific minority community needs can activate a credible challenger quickly if the incumbent's performance appears insufficient. Treating 2024's unopposed result as baseline security for 2026 and beyond is misreading the district's actual competitive dynamics and the sophistication of its evaluative electorate." }
