          { name: "Treating 62-38 as a Safe, Durable Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 result is a clear majority but not a supermajority or a commanding hold—it reflects a district where 38% of voters actively chose the opposition and whose preferences are responsive to economic messaging and candidate credibility. In a state where Republican lean is deep, 62% is a solid alignment, but in a district where one-third of households are renters and median age is 39, that 38% opposition is not inherently unstable or weak; it is concentrated among constituencies with material economic concerns that a credible challenger can address." },
          { name: "Assuming Working-Class Alignment with Conservative Economics", tag: "avoid", why: "A working-class, 35% renter, $65,901 median income constituency does not automatically embrace free-market economics or deregulation rhetoric—these households experience markets primarily as cost structures that pressure their budgets. Candidates who default to abstract conservative economic positioning without addressing concrete household concerns (rental affordability, healthcare costs, wage stagnation) will find this constituency more persuadable by populist economic messaging, regardless of partisan label. Economic credibility in working-class districts requires specific answers to specific material concerns, not ideology." }
