          { name: "Aging Homeowner Economic Security Under Fixed-Income Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.7 median age, 79.7% homeownership (inferred from 26.3% renter rate), and $54,767 median household income, NC-SD-47 is a district of older homeowners on constrained incomes facing accelerating fixed costs. Healthcare expenses, property tax burdens, rural hospital viability, and Medicare sustainability are not abstract policy issues — they are direct financial pressures on the constituency that elected Hise with 64%. A representative who addresses these material concerns with specificity and concrete solutions is building the relationship that makes the margin durable; one who assumes demographic stability without engagement risks finding that 36% opposition hardens into a credible challenge." },
          { name: "Rural Infrastructure Decline and Service Accessibility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's median income of $54,767 and college attainment of 28.2% in a rural North Carolina context signal a constituency for whom rural infrastructure — hospitals, broadband, water systems, roads — is not a luxury amenity but the foundation of daily life and economic viability. Rural service decline is accelerating across North Carolina's rural regions, and constituencies experiencing hospital closures, broadband gaps, and infrastructure decay are experiencing this as a direct threat to their quality of life and property values. Demonstrating active engagement with rural infrastructure preservation, healthcare access, and service sustainability is essential to justifying continued Republican support in a district aging in place." },
          { name: "Understanding the 36% Opposition in a Conservative District", tag: "careful", why: "A 36% Democratic share in a deeply red North Carolina district is not normal partisan baseline — it represents genuine frustration within a constituency that should, by demographic profile, be more reliably Republican. Whether driven by healthcare costs frustrating older voters, property tax burdens, rural service decline, or feeling ignored by a representative focused on statewide positioning, the 36% likely concentrates among the same aging fixed-income households that form the district's core. Identifying specifically what generated that opposition — and addressing it rather than dismissing it as inevitable Democratic noise — is the difference between a stable 64% and a vulnerable seat in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Treating 64% as an Indefinitely Safe Margin Without Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-36 margin is comfortable but not exceptional, and it reflects a constituency under real material stress whose patience for representation that ignores their economic security concerns is not infinite. Any representative who campaigns this seat as though 64% is a durable floor while neglecting the concrete concerns of aging homeowners — healthcare costs, rural service decline, property tax burden — is taking unnecessary risk. The margin will hold only if the representative actively engages the material concerns driving the opposition, not through passive confidence in demographics." },
          { name: "Assuming the District's Demographic Stability Means Political Stability", tag: "avoid", why: "NC-SD-47's demographic profile — 88.87% white, 43.7 median age, $54,767 income, 28.2% college attainment — is stable in absolute terms but not necessarily in political terms. Older, fixed-income homeowners facing healthcare cost inflation, rural service decline, and property tax increases are experiencing material stress that can shift voting behavior or increase opposition turnout even within demographically stable populations. Assuming that because the district looks the same demographically it will vote the same way electorally is a failure of political imagination. The 36% opposition exists because material conditions are creating frustration; ignoring those conditions in favor of celebrating demographic stability invites that 36% to harden into a credible alternative." }
