          { name: "Renter Mobilization and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 25.6%, renters represent one in four households in NC-SD-48 — a constituency that is substantially absent from the 64% Republican result and represents the most retrievable Democratic demographic in the district. In a $60,701 median-income district, renter households face significant housing cost burdens, and stagnant wages have made the renter constituency younger, more economically stressed, and less engaged in state legislative politics. A Democratic challenger who invests in genuine renter mobilization — focusing on housing affordability, rental protections, and cost-of-living support — can materially shift the margin from the 36% Democratic baseline, particularly if the incumbent has ignored this constituency's presence." },
          { name: "Small AIAN Population and Demographic Completeness", tag: "careful", why: "The ~3.36% AIAN gap in NC-SD-48 is the smallest in comparative analysis, meaning this district is demographically transparent and there are no large hidden populations whose political participation would substantially alter the margin. However, in districts with small Native American populations, the issue is visibility and service, not electoral significance — ensuring that the small AIAN community has genuine constituent access and that their governance concerns around land, water, and healthcare receive respectful attention is a basic representative obligation. A candidate who acknowledges and serves this small constituency demonstrates institutional integrity." },
          { name: "Treating Renters as an Inevitable Democratic Underclass", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 Democratic result of 36% in a district with 25.6% renters suggests that renters either did not vote at higher rates or voted Republican — not that renters are an inherently Republican constituency. Assuming renters are unrecoverable or are a fixed Democratic base that simply did not show up is analytically equivalent to surrendering the constituency without testing whether engagement and specific housing-affordability messaging would mobilize them. Any challenger who treats renters as predetermined losers rather than as a mobilizable constituency without active field investment is making a strategic choice to concede the seat." },
          { name: "Messaging 64% as an Inevitable Republican Result", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64% Republican result in a district with this demographic composition — 80.98% white, older, homeowner-heavy — is not an inevitable outcome; it is the result of a specific Republican strategy that maximizes homeowner turnout and consolidation while allowing renter participation to atrophy. Messaging this seat as though 64% represents durable Republican dominance rather than the outcome of deliberate incumbent strategy and renter disengagement obscures the actual vulnerability. A candidate who campaigns this district as though the margin is uncontestable will find the margin is exactly as stable as field investment makes it." }
