          "Engage renters on material economic security, not ideology: renters in a $60,701 median-income district face housing cost burdens, healthcare access challenges, and childcare affordability constraints that homeowners do not. A legislative agenda focused on these concrete household-budget pressures — not abstract progressivism — speaks to who actually lives here and why they might choose a different representative.",
          "Lead with community stability and cost-of-living governance: the 47.3-year-old, property-owning, middle-income constituency responds to candidates who take property taxes, healthcare costs, and rural service delivery seriously. A message that acknowledges real fiscal constraints rather than promising open-ended spending better reflects the demographic reality of who votes and what they care about.",
          "Avoid treating this as a 'red district' that cannot be contested: at 64%, this seat is safe for an incumbent who holds the homeowner coalition and maintains basic renter invisibility. But a challenger who invests in renter mobilization and creates genuine choice on cost-of-living issues can shift the margin meaningfully — particularly in a state where renter turnout is chronically suppressed."
